{"builder":"Goal: make one pull request that meets both acceptance lines of issue \"Read TSV too\":\n1. `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that `node bin/csv2json.js` prints for the same table as CSV.\n2. README.md documents --tsv.\n\nContext: src/parse.js already exports `parse(text, sep = ',')`, which splits each line on `sep`. bin/csv2json.js currently ignores its arguments and calls `parse(readFileSync(0, 'utf8'))`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether `process.argv.slice(2)` includes `--tsv`. If it does, call `parse(input, '\\t')`; if not, call `parse(input)` exactly as before. Keep the output format the same: `JSON.stringify(...)` followed by a newline. Running without the flag must behave exactly as it does now.\n2. In README.md, add a usage line such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` with a short sentence saying that --tsv reads tab-separated input instead of CSV. The literal text `--tsv` must appear in README.md.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a test that runs `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` with tab-separated input such as `'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n'` and asserts the output equals `'[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'`, the same output the existing CSV cli test expects. Keep the existing tests.\n4. Run `npm test` and make sure every test passes. Also run this and confirm it prints SAME:\n   diff <(printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nAda\\t36\\tLondon\\nBob\\t41\\tParis\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv) <(printf 'name,age,city\\nAda,36,London\\nBob,41,Paris\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js) && echo SAME\n5. Commit the changes and open the pull request.","verifier":"Run every command from the repository root at the PR head, in bash.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun:\n  printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nAda\\t36\\tLondon\\nBob\\t41\\tParis\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; printf 'name,age,city\\nAda,36,London\\nBob,41,Paris\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js; diff <(printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nAda\\t36\\tLondon\\nBob\\t41\\tParis\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv) <(printf 'name,age,city\\nAda,36,London\\nBob,41,Paris\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js) && echo SAME\nPASS: both commands exit 0, the two printed outputs are identical, the CSV output is the JSON array [{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"age\":\"36\",\"city\":\"London\"},{\"name\":\"Bob\",\"age\":\"41\",\"city\":\"Paris\"}], and the last line is SAME.\nFAIL: either command errors, the outputs differ (diff prints lines and SAME does not appear), or the --tsv output keeps tabs inside keys or values, as in {\"name\\tage\\tcity\":...}.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRun:\n  grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md\nPASS: at least one matching line is printed, and the matching text explains that --tsv makes the tool read tab-separated input, for example with a usage line.\nFAIL: grep prints nothing (exit status 1), or `--tsv` appears in README.md without saying what it does.\n\nAdvisory (does not decide PASS or FAIL):\n- Run `npm test`. All tests, the existing ones included, should pass. Note any failure.\n- Check that running `node bin/csv2json.js` without --tsv still behaves as before. Also check whether the PR adds a test for --tsv."}